publish: allow — cargo-vet's imported audit records.

`imports.lock` is written wholesale by `cargo vet`, from audit files published
by Mozilla, Google and the other importers named in `supply-chain/config.toml`.
Every address it carries sits in a `who = "Name <address>"` field and belongs to
the UPSTREAM AUDITOR who signed that record — it is their public attestation,
already published at the source this file mirrors, and it is the attribution
that makes the record worth anything. None of it is operator identity, which is
what check F exists to keep out of the public projection.

The inline `publish: allow` marker cannot be used here, and that is the only
reason this sidecar exists: the next `cargo vet` run rewrites the file and
erases every marker in it, so the waiver would silently stop existing while the
addresses stayed. The blob pin below does the job the marker cannot — refresh
the imports and the pin lapses, the gate reds, and a human re-reads what the
refresh brought in before restating the hash.

To restate it after a legitimate refresh:

    git hash-object -- supply-chain/imports.lock

